# Auto commit & push

After finishing a **substantial** change (feature, bugfix, multi-file edit, config flip — not a one-line typo or a pure Q&A), do this in the same turn without waiting for the user to ask:

1. `git status` / `git diff` / recent `git log` (parallel)
2. Stage relevant files only (never `.env`, secrets, credentials)
3. Commit with a **short message in Latin script only** (English preferred)
4. `git push origin HEAD` (or the current tracking branch)
5. Confirm with commit hash / that push succeeded

## Commit message rules

- Latin characters only (no Cyrillic)
- Short: ~1 line, ideally under 72 chars
- Imperative mood: `Disable phone auth temporarily`, not `Disabled...`
- Focus on why/what in one phrase; no multi-paragraph bodies unless needed for safety

Examples:

```
Disable phone auth for open room join
Update footer copyright text
Fix open join when phone auth is off
```

## Do not auto-commit when

- User is only asking a question / reviewing / exploring
- Working tree has unrelated WIP the user did not ask to include
- Change is trivial (typo, comment-only) unless user asked to ship it
- Commit would include secrets

## Safety

- Never `--force` push to `main`/`master`
- Never amend a commit that was already pushed
- Never update git config
- Never skip hooks unless user explicitly asks
